  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting ready for a porcelain tile installation might seem daunting, but breaking it down into a few steps makes it much easier. First off, you've gotta make sure your subfloor is in tip-top shape. That means it needs to be super clean, smooth, completely dry, and perfectly level. Any bumps or dips can cause issues later, like tiles cracking or an uneven surface. Next, planning your layout is key. You'll want to snap some reference lines to guide your tile placement, making sure everything stays straight and even. Don't forget about having the right spacers on hand; they're essential for consistent grout lines. Once the tiles are set and the adhesive has had plenty of time to cure—we're talking around 24 hours before any foot traffic—then you can move on to grouting. After grouting, give it another good 72 hours before subjecting it to heavy use. This allows everything to bond properly and creates a durable, lasting surface. If you're unsure about any of these steps, especially with tricky areas like wet rooms or large formats, it's always smart to bring in a professional. HH Remodeling LLC can help you get it right."
    }
  ],
